{"results":[{"url":"https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/scfindex.htm","final_url":"https://www.federalreserve.gov/econres/scfindex.htm","title":"Survey of Consumer Finances (SCF)","description":"The Federal Reserve Board of Governors in Washington DC.","language":"en","text":"## Survey of Consumer Finances (SCF)\n\nThe 2025 Survey of Consumer Finances (SCF) is the most recent survey conducted. Below are links to the report, interactive chartbook, historical report tables, full public dataset, extract dataset, replicate weight files, and technical documents.\n\n#### Summary Results\n\n**Changes in U.S. Family Finances from 2022 to 2025: Evidence from the Survey of Consumer Finances**  \nPDF  \nOctober 9, 2026\n\nStandard Error Tables (Excel)\n\n#### SCF Interactive Chart\n\nView Interactive Chartbook\n\nMore information\n\n#### Historic Tables\n\nThe following tables are based on those that have historically appeared in the main article. Estimates for all survey years from 1989 to the most recent survey year are included in both nominal and real terms.\n\n##### Historic Tables Based on Internal and External Data\n\nMake Full Screen\n\nHistoric Tables Based on Internal and External Data\n\n| File Type | File | Documentation | Last Updated |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Excel Based on Internal Data | Summary Extract Data Program (TXT) | 10/9/2026 |\n| Excel Based on Public Data | 10/9/2026 |\n\nMore information\n\n#### Survey Data and Replicate Weight Files\n\n##### Full Public Data Set - Full data sets of all SCF variables.\n\nMake Full Screen\n\nFull Public Data Set Files by Format\n\n| File Type | Main Survey Data | Replicate Weight File (X42001) | Documentation | Last Updated |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n\n**WARNING:** Please review the following PDF for instructions on how to calculate correct standard errors. As a result of multiple imputation, the dataset you are downloading contains five times the number of actual observations. Failure to account for the imputations and the complex sample design will result in incorrect estimation of standard errors.\n\nMore information\n\n***Special note to R users:*** An outside programmer has created scripts for converting and working with SCF data. These scripts are available for download from: https://guilhermejacob.github.io/context/1.6-survey-of-consumer-finances-scf.html#survey-of-consumer-finances-scf\n\n##### Summary Extract Public Data - Summary variables used in the Federal Reserve Report – all dollar variables inflation-adjusted to 2025 dollars.\n\nMake Full Screen\n\nSummary Extract Public Data Files by Format\n\n| File Type | File | Documentation | Last Updated |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| SDA online analysis | SDA analysis tool | 10/9/2026 |\n| SAS | SAS extract data (3 MB ZIP) | 10/9/2026 |\n| STATA | Stata extract data (3 MB ZIP) | 10/9/2026 |\n| CSV | CSV extract data (2 MB ZIP) | 10/9/2026 |\n\nMore information\n\n#### Technical Documents\n\nMake Full Screen\n\nTechnical Documentation Files\n\n| File | Last Updated |\n| --- | --- |\n| Codebook (2.7 MB ASCII) | 10/9/2026 |\n| Showcards (PDF) | 10/9/2026 |\n| Questionnaire Outline (PDF) | 10/9/2026 |\n| Simulation of MR Interview Program (1 MB DOCX) | 10/9/2026 |\n| Changes for 2025 (PDF) | 10/9/2026 |\n| Net Worth Flowchart (PDF) | 10/9/2026 |\n\nMore information\n\n##### Have A Question About The SCF\n\n* To get updates to these surveys\n* Sign our guest book\n* To send a comment or make technical inquiries\n* Ask Us\n\n#### Missing Data\n\nMissing data in the survey have been imputed five times using a multiple imputation technique. The information is stored in five separate imputation replicates (implicates). Thus, for the 4,367 families interviewed for the survey, there are 21,835 records in the data set. Seven observations were deleted for the public version of the data set for purposes of disclosure avoidance; thus, there are 21,800 records in the public data set for 4,360 families. The codebook provides more detail on the structure of the data set and the steps taken for disclosure avoidance.\n\n##### Citation\n\n**URL**\n**DOI Identifier**:   https://doi.org/10.17016/8799.1\n**Creator**:   Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve Board\n**Name**:   2025 Survey of Consumer Finances\n**Description**:   The Survey of Consumer Finances (SCF) is normally a triennial cross-sectional survey of U.S. families. The survey data include information on families' balance sheets, pensions, income, and demographic characteristics.\n**Publisher**:   Board of Governors of the Federal Reserve System\n**Publication Year**:   2026\n\nLast Update:\n10/09/2026","author":null,"published_date":null,"latency_ms":1819.933858999775,"format":"markdown"}],"errors":[]}